@@ -57,6 +57,58 @@ zsh alert-hook.sh --list          # every name, its game, clip and sprite
 zsh alert-hook.sh --play tesla    # hear it now; touches no pane
 ```
 
+**Doom face.** A Claude pane gets the classic Doom status-bar face drawn in its
+corner, picked from how much of that session's context window is used up —
+calm when it's fresh, worse through the same health bands the real status bar
+uses, dead when it's about to compact. There's no herdr event for token usage,
+so `bin/herdr-doomface` polls the session's own transcript JSONL for its most
+recent `usage` object (`doomface-hook.sh` starts one such loop per Claude pane,
+tracked by pidfile so the same pane doesn't get a second one on its next status
+change) and draws over the socket the same way the alert flash does — a
+graphics layer composited above the pane's cells, so a full-screen agent
+repainting its background never erases it. The frames themselves come from
+id Software's own freely-redistributed v1.9 shareware IWAD, verified by hash
+before anything is cut out of it; see `scripts/fetch-doom-wad.py` for exactly
+what that means and why it's a different legal footing than the git-repo
+sprite rips the alert sprites use. Turn it off entirely, or stop whatever's
+already running, without waiting for a pane to close:
+
+```sh
+HERDR_DOOMFACE_OFF=1              # in config.sh — never start the face
+zsh doomface-hook.sh --stop-all   # or the "Stop all doom faces" action
+```
+
+The corner sprite only exists inside that one pane's own cells. To see the
+same face in Herdr's own sidebar instead (or as well), the loop also reports
+`$doomface` (an emoji standing in for the sprite) and `$doomface_pct` through
+`pane.report-metadata` — but per Herdr's docs, a metadata reporter only
+supplies the value; a sidebar row still has to name the token before it draws
+anything. Add it to your own `~/.config/herdr/config.toml` and reload config
+(Herdr's global menu, not `herdr server reload-config` — sidebar layout is
+client-local):
+
+```toml
+[ui.sidebar.agents.rows_by_agent]
+claude = [
+  ["state_icon", "machine", "workspace", "tab"],
+  ["agent", "$doomface"],
+]
+```
+
+Neither of those is a real sprite where you're actually looking, though — the
+corner overlay is real pixels but stuck to one tiny patch of one specific
+pane's cells, and the sidebar can only ever show text. For the actual face,
+full-size, sitting in its own place: the **"Open Doom face widget" action**
+splits off a dedicated pane — `bin/herdr-doomface-widget` running in a real,
+ordinary Herdr pane it owns outright, drawing straight to its own tty with no
+socket RPC involved. `placement = "split"` rather than `popup` on purpose:
+Herdr's docs are explicit that popups are session-modal and steal all
+terminal input, where split/tab/zoomed panes are normal panes that just sit
+there until closed — the difference between a dialog and a widget. It tracks
+whichever pane was active when you ran the action (`HERDR_DOOMFACE_TARGET`,
+set by `doomface-widget-action.sh`), not itself, and closes the same way any
+other pane does.
+
 **Layouts.** `layout.export` and `layout.apply` are first-class socket methods
 with a published schema, but `herdr layout` is not a command. `herdr-layout-save`
 and `herdr-layout-load` are that missing CLI. Structure only: splits, labels,
